test_poisson_octree -- createpts / poisson_disk / 3d_box with h_lookup octree
=======================================================================

QUICK START

  sh run_poisson_octree_tests.sh [path-to-lagrit]    10 decks, about 15 s
  open summary.html

  The page explains each deck and check, and shows:
    - a slice of po01's octree through the point source, coloured by
      level, with the vertices near that plane
    - po01's spacing: nearest-neighbour distance / h, and the nearest-
      neighbour distance against distance from the source, with h
    - octree (po01) against hex_grid (po07) on the same field: time,
      peak memory, points h is looked up at, vertices, tets, and the
      smallest and largest dihedral angles of the tets
    - constant h (po06), octree against hex_grid: spacing, and the
      vertices near the plane z = 0.5 side by side
    - po01's octree in numbers (leaves per level, passes, batches,
      memory) and its maximality report
  Each deck's time and peak memory are recorded by the checker's
  "run" step (work/<deck>.rusage).

WHAT IS TESTED

  h_lookup octree looks h up in an octree adapted to h, instead of a
  uniform lattice over the box, and samples on a neighbour grid made
  of the octree's leaves, with a maximal fill. The checks are rules and
  ranges, not reference meshes: random numbers differ between C++
  libraries, so exact coordinates differ between Linux and macOS.

  Every deck: LaGriT finishes, and no ERROR in its output.

THE DECKS

  The unit box, edge buffer off, h_spacing_scalar 0.02, and the h field
  h_point.mlgi: h = 0.02 + 0.2 * distance to (0.3, 0.6, 0.45), at most
  0.2 (po01 to po04, po07).

  po01_point_source   700 to 1000 vertices; interior vertices at least
                      max(h) apart (h computed here from the formula,
                      not looked up); vertices on the box faces at
                      least 0.6 max(h) from any other (faces are
                      sampled with 0.9 h, box edges with 0.81 h); the
                      maximality report leaves under 1e-3 of the
                      volume; octree_dump writes as many cells and
                      vertices as the run reports
  po02_small_batch    octree_batch 50: the h field runs on more than 50
                      batches, and the vertices are the same as po01's
  po03_other_seed     poisson_seed 2: other vertices, a count within 5%
  po04_k_factor       octree_k_factor 1.5: more leaves than po01; count
                      within 5%; interior vertices at least max(h) apart
  po05_read_octree    reads po01's octree file back: every hex cell has
                      a positive volume; h_field_att at least 0.02
  po07_point_hex_grid po01 with h_lookup hex_grid: interior vertices at
                      least max(h) apart; po01 makes at least 0.95 times
                      its vertices, with less peak memory
  po06_constant_h_*   h = 0.1 everywhere, no h field file, with
                      h_lookup octree and with hex_grid: interior
                      vertices at least h apart in both; the octree
                      makes 1.0 to 1.45 times hex_grid's vertices (its
                      fill is maximal; hex_grid's cheap cell test
                      rejects more than its rule says)

  po08_default_graded  po01 without h_lookup: with a control file the
                      default is the octree (it says so); the
                      vertices are exactly po01's
  po09_default_uniform po06 without h_lookup: for a uniform h the
                      default is hex_grid (it says so; the mesh of
                      LaGriT before V3.340); the vertices are exactly
                      po06_constant_h_hex_grid's

  The error and warning cases of the new keywords are in
  test_poisson_command_errors (err26 to err31, warn06).

FILES

  h_point.mlgi              the h field of po01 to po04 and po07
  po*.lgi                   the decks
  check_poisson_octree.py   the checker (Python 3, standard library)
  run_poisson_octree_tests.sh
  work/, logs/              written by each run (not tracked)
